    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="241b7dd8-4648-566a-8e3c-4bbfeb9c1234" activityNameId="83d18510-13c2-598d-8b07-de4121d4156d" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Hydrated lime production, from quicklime with carbon capture and storage</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Hydrated lime production, from quicklime with carbon capture and storage
The dataset supplies 1 kilogram of hydrated lime, produced with quicklime that has been produce with carbon capture and storage. This dataset accounts for the energy and water input for hydration. The low-grade heat that is generated during CaO hydration is typically not recovered and therefore excluded from the system boundaries, (around 1.2 GJ per ton CaO (EuLA, 2014). The infrastructure for quicklime hydration is assumed to be shared with the one for quicklime production, i.e., the LCI data for a cement factory cover both stages. Source: Foteinis, S., Andresen, J., Campo, F., Caserini, S., &amp; Renforth, P. (2022). Life cycle assessment of ocean liming for carbon dioxide removal from the atmosphere. Journal of Cleaner Production, 370, 133309. https://doi.org/10.1016/J.JCLEPRO.2022.133309
UUID: c6c9adf3-8958-3fce-98b4-363e59681669
Location: 
Technology: 
Time period: 
Production volume: 
Sampling: Sampling procedure: Literature data
Extrapolations: 
Uncertainty adjustments: 
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_c6c9adf3-8958-3fce-98b4-363e59681669.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
